{{ request }}

Also write a `rationale`: ONE plain sentence on WHY this requirement matters pedagogically (the benefit to students or to teaching), in the professor's language. It is shown next to the rule and used by the report to explain the point of a fix. Do not restate the rule; give its reason.

Before you answer, check your own aggregation, this is where these rules most often go wrong. Read the request again and name its quantifier (presence "at least one" / violation "no page" / universal "every page" / coverage "states every …, comprehensively" / proportion "most" / relative-positional "X must follow/precede Y", "within N pages/slides of Y" / absolute-positional "the last/first page", "at the end", "the final third" → `pages` field plus `doc.page_count` arithmetic, never an extracted page-total field). Then ask: with the agg I chose, could a document satisfy this rule in a way the professor would consider WRONG, e.g. one page passing a rule that asked about the whole course, or a formula and an example both present but pages apart when the rule wanted them adjacent? If yes, your aggregation is too weak: switch a coverage/sufficiency requirement to a count with a threshold, "any" to "all", or a positional requirement to `pages` fields with a macro, before you reply. Finally re-check applicability: a precondition that controls the whole rule MUST be a gate, while a conditional clause inside a rule with independent requirements MUST NOT be one. Mechanical check: if the entire CEL starts with `doc.<field>.all(`, `<field>` MUST be a gate; if that macro is combined with an independent top-level requirement, it MUST NOT be. Do this reasoning silently; output only the final JSON.

Reply with a single JSON object and nothing else:
{"description": "<one clear sentence stating the requirement, in the professor's language>", "rationale": "<one sentence on why it matters>", "cel": "<the CEL expression over doc>", "severity": "high|medium|low", "category": "outcomes|assessment|ai_readiness|content|clarity", "contract": [<one entry per field used by the expression>]}
